00:10:25,440 --> 00:10:30,080
in this network, but Wilma realized she

276
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,720
could do something that was nearly as

277
00:10:30,080 --> 00:10:33,760
good. What if you took pictures of

278
00:10:31,720 --> 00:10:35,400
ordinary people, like the ones Wilma

279
00:10:33,760 --> 00:10:39,200
showed her volunteers in that early

280
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:40,839
study. You ask an AI to make a guess

281
00:10:39,200 --> 00:10:43,120
about how memorable the first face is

282
00:10:40,839 --> 00:10:45,720
going to be. It makes a random guess. If

283
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:48,520
it guessed the face was memorable, and

284
00:10:45,720 --> 00:10:49,960
humans also find the face memorable, you

285
00:10:48,520 --> 00:10:52,400
tell the AI,

286
00:10:49,960 --> 00:10:54,839
"Good job. Keep doing what you're doing.

287
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:57,120
Strengthen the connections you used to

288
00:10:54,839 --> 00:10:59,720
make this guess." If it guesses a face

289
00:10:57,120 --> 00:11:02,440
was memorable, but humans think it

290
00:10:59,720 --> 00:11:04,720
isn't, you tell the AI, "You got that

291
00:11:02,440 --> 00:11:06,120
wrong. Whatever connections you used to

292
00:11:04,720 --> 00:11:08,640
make that guess,

293
00:11:06,120 --> 00:11:11,680
weaken them." Do this often enough, over

294
00:11:08,640 --> 00:11:14,280
and over again, and eventually, the AI

295
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:16,600
will start to learn the secret patterns

296
00:11:14,280 --> 00:11:18,320
that humans are using to determine

297
00:11:16,600 --> 00:11:20,040
whether something is memorable. What

298
00:11:18,320 --> 00:11:23,200
you've done, of course, is you've built

299
00:11:20,040 --> 00:11:24,839
an AI model for memorability. You still

300
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:26,680
don't understand what's happening inside

301
00:11:24,839 --> 00:11:28,160
the human brain, and you might not

302
00:11:26,680 --> 00:11:31,400
understand everything that's happening

303
00:11:28,160 --> 00:11:34,240
inside the AI, but now you can use the

304
00:11:31,400 --> 00:11:37,360
AI model to make predictions about how

305
00:11:34,240 --> 00:11:39,400
memorable a new face is going to be. If

306
00:11:37,360 --> 00:11:41,560
the AI has learned well, it will tell

307
00:11:39,400 --> 00:11:43,880
you whether humans will find a face

308
00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:46,080
memorable. Wilma and her team built an

309
00:11:43,880 --> 00:11:50,400
artificial intelligence model to do just

310
00:11:46,080 --> 00:11:50,400
this. They called it ResMem.

311
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:54,720
You can upload a picture and it'll tell

312
00:11:52,840 --> 00:11:57,440
you how memorable that picture is. So,

313
00:11:54,720 --> 00:11:59,520
like one means 100% of people remember

314
00:11:57,440 --> 00:12:02,000
it while zero means no one will remember

315
00:11:59,520 --> 00:12:05,240
it. They trained this AI on regular

316
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:07,280
photographs, faces, scenes, everyday

317
00:12:05,240 --> 00:12:09,760
objects. They didn't train it on art

318
00:12:07,280 --> 00:12:12,600
history. This AI knew nothing about

319
00:12:09,760 --> 00:12:14,960
Picasso. It didn't know who Van Gogh is.

320
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:17,680
It has no idea what a masterpiece is

321
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:19,400
supposed to look like. Wilma put the AI

322
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:21,960
to the ultimate test. She took the

323
00:12:19,400 --> 00:12:24,600
paintings from the Art Institute study

324
00:12:21,960 --> 00:12:27,200
and fed them into ResMem. And she asked,

325
00:12:24,600 --> 00:12:29,080
"Is this painting memorable?" Some of

326
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:32,360
these paintings were famous works, the

327
00:12:29,080 --> 00:12:34,680
ones printed mugs and tote bags. Others

328
00:12:32,360 --> 00:12:36,560
were obscure works by the same artists

329
00:12:34,680 --> 00:12:39,800
or from the same era that never became

330
00:12:36,560 --> 00:12:41,680
famous. The result was staggering. When

331
00:12:39,800 --> 00:12:44,280
asked which paintings were going to be

332
00:12:41,680 --> 00:12:46,600
more memorable, the AI tended to pick

333
00:12:44,280 --> 00:12:48,960
the paintings that humans considered

334
00:12:46,600 --> 00:12:51,600
masterpieces. And what we were surprised

335
00:12:48,960 --> 00:12:53,760
to see was that ResMem predicted that

336
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:57,080
famous pieces were more memorable than

337
00:12:53,760 --> 00:12:57,080
non-famous pieces.

338
00:12:59,040 --> 00:13:03,240
Think about what this means. American

339
00:13:00,839 --> 00:13:05,400
Gothic isn't just famous because critics

340
00:13:03,240 --> 00:13:07,280
liked it and a bunch of humans behaved

341
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:08,839
like sheep and followed them. It isn't

342
00:13:07,280 --> 00:13:10,720
just famous because it captures

343
00:13:08,839 --> 00:13:13,680
something about the American spirit.

344
00:13:10,720 --> 00:13:16,160
It's famous because Grant Wood happened

345
00:13:13,680 --> 00:13:18,880
to arrange the visual information in a

346
00:13:16,160 --> 00:13:21,280
way that hacked the human brain. He

347
00:13:18,880 --> 00:13:24,360
created an image that was cognitively

348
00:13:21,280 --> 00:13:27,240
sticky. The AI, blind to culture and

349
00:13:24,360 --> 00:13:29,400
history, was identifying masterpieces by

350
00:13:27,240 --> 00:13:31,960
looking at the pixels. It seemed to be

351
00:13:29,400 --> 00:13:34,360
identifying the visual hook that makes a

352
00:13:31,960 --> 00:13:37,240
painting turn into a memory. And that

353
00:13:34,360 --> 00:13:40,080
memory turn into fame. So, does this

354
00:13:37,240 --> 00:13:41,880
mean that art is just an algorithm? That

355
00:13:40,080 --> 00:13:43,520
we can paint by numbers to create the

356
00:13:41,880 --> 00:13:46,120
next Mona Lisa?

357
00:13:43,520 --> 00:13:48,680
Not exactly. Res nom can tell you if an

358
00:13:46,120 --> 00:13:50,960
image is memorable, but it cannot tell

359
00:13:48,680 --> 00:13:53,160
you if it has meaning.

360
00:13:50,960 --> 00:13:54,680
A photo of a car crash might be highly

361
00:13:53,160 --> 00:13:56,520
memorable, but you wouldn't want it

362
00:13:54,680 --> 00:13:58,480
hanging over your sofa. But this

363
00:13:56,520 --> 00:14:01,760
research does challenge the romantic

364
00:13:58,480 --> 00:14:04,040
idea that art is mysterious or purely

365
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:07,120
subjective. It suggests that there are

366
00:14:04,040 --> 00:14:09,320
deep, ancient currents in our brains

367
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:11,840
that dictate which images make a lasting

368
00:14:09,320 --> 00:14:14,720
impression. There is a formula for

369
00:14:11,840 --> 00:14:14,720
masterpieces.

370
00:14:15,640 --> 00:14:20,440
I need to mention one final, profound

371
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:22,920
application for this very nascent

372
00:14:20,440 --> 00:14:25,240
technology. It goes beyond art. It

373
00:14:22,920 --> 00:14:27,640
speaks to the very fragility of the

374
00:14:25,240 --> 00:14:29,400
human mind. While healthy brains tend to

375
00:14:27,640 --> 00:14:31,600
remember similar images, people

376
00:14:29,400 --> 00:14:33,600
suffering from early cognitive decline,

377
00:14:31,600 --> 00:14:35,800
like Alzheimer's disease, start to

378
00:14:33,600 --> 00:14:38,000
diverge from healthy people when it

379
00:14:35,800 --> 00:14:40,080
comes to identifying memorable images.

380
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:41,960
We published a study a few years ago

381
00:14:40,080 --> 00:14:43,720
where we found that there are some

382
00:14:41,960 --> 00:14:45,800
special images that are memorable to

383
00:14:43,720 --> 00:14:47,520
healthy people, but forgettable if

384
00:14:45,800 --> 00:14:49,760
you're in the early stages of

385
00:14:47,520 --> 00:14:52,240
Alzheimer's disease. Researchers might

386
00:14:49,760 --> 00:14:54,240
one day use these images like canaries

387
00:14:52,240 --> 00:14:56,480
in a coal mine. If people remember

388
00:14:54,240 --> 00:14:58,680
certain images, but your friend forgets

389
00:14:56,480 --> 00:15:00,680
them, it could be a warning sign that

390
00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:02,560
something is wrong in her brain. We

391
00:15:00,680 --> 00:15:04,800
often feel that our memories are a

392
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:07,280
recording of our lives, but Wilma

393
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:10,040
Bainbridge's work suggests that what we

394
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:12,800
remember and what we forget are shaped

395
00:15:10,040 --> 00:15:15,080
by our own histories and preferences and

396
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:17,560
knowledge. Whether it's the false memory

397
00:15:15,080 --> 00:15:19,120
of a monocle on the Monopoly Man or the

398
00:15:17,560 --> 00:15:22,000
enduring fame of a Van Gogh

399
00:15:19,120 --> 00:15:25,600
self-portrait, our minds are constantly

400
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:27,960
curating our reality based on rules we

401
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:29,720
are only just beginning to understand.

402
00:15:27,960 --> 00:15:31,680
So the next time you create something, a

403
00:15:29,720 --> 00:15:34,400
painting, a photograph, even a

404
00:15:31,680 --> 00:15:36,920
presentation for work, remember you are

405
00:15:34,400 --> 00:15:39,400
not just fighting for attention. You are

406
00:15:36,920 --> 00:15:42,080
fighting to be remembered in the brains

407
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:43,120
of other people. And yes, the fight is

408
00:15:42,080 --> 00:15:45,240
rigged.

409
00:15:43,120 --> 00:15:47,160
But at least now you know something

410
00:15:45,240 --> 00:15:49,440
about the rules. If you want to test

411
00:15:47,160 --> 00:15:51,760
your own creations or just see how your

412
00:15:49,440 --> 00:15:53,600
memory stacks up against the machine,

413
00:15:51,760 --> 00:15:55,640
Wilma Bainbridge has made the ResMem

414
00:15:53,600 --> 00:15:57,760
tool public. You can check it out at

